//? Title: Horspool Search: The Window-Ending Byte Chooses the Shift
//? Subtitle: Actual alignment records, prefix-only shift tables and literal queries over passenger names
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Horspool simplifies Boyer–Moore by using one shift table keyed by the text
byte at the end of the current pattern window. That byte can differ from the
one that caused a mismatch. We illustrate the distinction, retain the original
abra search and shift-table plot, and inspect actual native alignment records.
Exact comparison counts show both large skips and a quadratic mismatch family.
Finally, built-in Titanic passenger names provide a record-wise text-search
example against an independent naive baseline. Logical work counts describe
these inputs; they do not establish a universal runtime ranking.
)

\toc

> A small example: mismatching X, but shifting on A

Search for ABCA in AXCABCA. At the first window, the ending A and preceding
C match, then B mismatches text byte X at pattern index one. Horspool uses
the window's ending A to choose shift three. It does not use the mismatching
X, whose table entry is four. The next window starts at offset three and
matches ABCA. The two windows cost three and four comparisons respectively.

The diagram reads those decisions from the same core scan as ordinary search.
Red cells are failed comparisons, green cells matched, and the blue outline
marks the text byte used for the shift lookup. Other cells were not compared
at that alignment.

```{cpp, file="figures/horspool_window_end.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string text="AXCABCA",pattern="ABCA";
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::boyer_moore_horspool_trace(text,pattern);
  dm::graphics::Diagram diagram(65,1100);
  for (std::size_t panel=0;panel<trace.alignments.size();++panel) {
    const auto& step=trace.alignments[panel];const double dx=11*panel;
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle cell;cell.width=.8;cell.height=.8;cell.padding=5;
    diagram.text({dx+3,2.2},std::format("s = {}; {} comparisons",step.position,step.comparisons));
    for (std::size_t i=0;i<text.size();++i) {
      cell.fill={239,246,255};cell.border={51,65,85};
      const bool compared=i>=step.position+pattern.size()-step.comparisons && i<step.position+pattern.size();
      if (compared) cell.fill=!step.matched&&i==step.position+step.mismatch_index?dm::RGB{254,226,226}:dm::RGB{220,252,231};
      if (i==step.position+pattern.size()-1) cell.border={37,99,235};
      diagram.node(std::format("t{}-{}",panel,i),{dx+i,0},std::string(1,text[i]),cell)
          .text({dx+i,1},std::format("{}",i),14);
    }
    for (std::size_t i=0;i<pattern.size();++i) {
      cell.fill={239,246,255};cell.border={51,65,85};
      if (i>=pattern.size()-step.comparisons)
        cell.fill=!step.matched&&i==step.mismatch_index?dm::RGB{254,226,226}:dm::RGB{220,252,231};
      diagram.node(std::format("p{}-{}",panel,i),{dx+step.position+i,-1.5},std::string(1,pattern[i]),cell);
    }
    diagram.text({dx+3,-3.1},std::format("{}; ending {} chooses shift {}",
        step.matched?"full match":"mismatch X",char(step.ending_byte),step.shift));
  }
  diagram.save_svg("build/horspool_window_end.svg");
}
```
// result_begin:
// \image(figures/horspool_window_end.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The final window also records its departure shift, although it leaves the set of possible starts. All coordinates count bytes.)
\alttext(ABCA aligns under AXCA. Its final CA matches, B fails against X, and the final text A selects a three-position shift. The next alignment at three matches ABCA completely.)

> The table and why the shift is safe

For a nonempty pattern P of length m, initialize every byte's shift to m.
For each position i<m-1, overwrite shift[P[i]] with m-1-i. Later positions
win, so the result uses the rightmost occurrence in the prefix that excludes
the final pattern byte:

$$
\operatorname{shift}[c]=
\begin{cases}
 m-1-\max\{i<m-1:P[i]=c\}, & c\text{ occurs in }P[0..m-2],\\
 m, & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(A byte's shift is its distance from the pattern end to the rightmost occurrence before the final position, or the full pattern length when none exists.)

Let c be text[s+m-1]. Any occurrence starting between s+1 and the proposed
next start would put c at a pattern position to the right of its rightmost
eligible prefix occurrence. That cannot match. If c has no prefix occurrence,
all starts before s+m are excluded. This argument depends on the final window
byte rather than on where the right-to-left comparisons stopped, and also
applies after a full match.

Excluding the final pattern position guarantees positive shifts and preserves
overlapping occurrences. For pattern aa, shift[a]=1, so searching aaaa finds
starts zero, one and two. For a one-byte pattern every shift is one. Empty
patterns match nowhere by this library's convention; their all-zero table is
never used to advance a search.

> Original example: four occurrences of abra

Retain text abracadabra_abra_cadabra and pattern abra. The final a is excluded
from preprocessing; the earlier a at index zero gives shift three. The table
below comes from `horspool_shifts`, replacing the original local table code.
The trace and ordinary function use the same scan kernel and retain the
original four matches at offsets 0, 7, 12 and 20.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/boyer_moore_horspool.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::string text="abracadabra_abra_cadabra",pattern="abra";
  const auto trace=dm::algorithms::boyer_moore_horspool_trace(text,pattern);
  const auto shifts=dm::algorithms::horspool_shifts(pattern);
  if (trace.scan.positions!=dm::algorithms::boyer_moore_horspool_search(text,pattern)) return 1;
  std::vector<double> heights;std::vector<std::string> labels;
  std::print("| window-ending byte | shift |\n| :--- | ---: |\n");
  for (char c:std::string("abr_z")) {
    const auto shift=shifts[static_cast<unsigned char>(c)];heights.push_back(shift);labels.emplace_back(1,c);
    std::print("| {} | {} |\n",c,shift);
  }
  std::print("\n| start | equal suffix bytes | result | ending byte | comparisons | shift |\n"
             "| ---: | ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for (const auto& step:trace.alignments)
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",step.position,step.matched_suffix_length,
        step.matched?"full match":std::format("mismatch j={}",step.mismatch_index),char(step.ending_byte),step.comparisons,step.shift);
  std::print("\nMatches: {}. Visited windows: {}; search comparisons: {}.\n",
      trace.scan.positions,trace.scan.alignments,trace.scan.comparisons);
  dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(heights,labels,"shift",dm::RGB{37,99,235})
      .title("Horspool shifts for abra: the final a is excluded")
      .x_label("byte at the end of the text window").y_label("next shift, bytes")
      .y_limits(0,6).size(900,480).save_svg("build/boyer_moore_horspool.svg");
}
```
