// result_end
\caption(All patterns are searched in the same records. The naive scan supplies an independent matching baseline; the bar chart compares total byte tests for this selected workload.)
\alttext(A bar chart contrasts the total search byte comparisons from naive search and Horspool over five literal passenger-name queries.)

The one-shot function takes O(m+256) preprocessing work and O(256) auxiliary
storage, plus O(K) returned offsets. Search is O((n-m+1)m) comparisons in the
worst eligible case. The trace retains O(A+K+256) storage for A actual windows
and K matches, without copying the pattern or text. Calls borrow inputs
without mutation and own their outputs; independent calls share no state.
The comparison counter is checked for size_t overflow. A larger shift on one
window is useful evidence about that window, while performance conclusions
need the actual workload, preprocessing costs and timing context.

> Reference

R. N. Horspool (1980), Practical fast searching in strings, Software: Practice
and Experience 10(6), 501–506. [Original paper](https://doi.org/10.1002/spe.4380100608).
The core uses right-to-left comparisons and extends the search to report every
overlapping occurrence.
